        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "feat(teams): add exact-tab workspace sharing",
        "body": "Refs #105322.\n\nBuilds on the Workspaces foundation landed in #104139.\n\n## What Problem This Solves\n\nOpenClaw's operator dashboard is intentionally single-operator and privileged. Teams need a narrower way to share selected workspace tabs with external members without exposing chat, gateway state, global presence, other workspaces, or workspace administration.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\n- add opt-in, gateway-local Teams accounts, opaque sessions, one-time invites, and a scope-less `member` role\n- add exact resource RBAC, domain isolation, server-attested agent delegation, and host-bound plugin Teams primitives\n- partition Workspaces by isolation domain and support exact-tab read, request-change, and write access\n- add a restricted `/teams` portal with owner invite/review controls and no operator dashboard navigation or global gateway state\n\n### Security and privacy boundaries\n\n- member sockets receive no gateway-global presence, health, defaults, chat, or plugin broadcasts\n- remote plaintext Teams authentication is rejected; loopback HTTP remains an explicit local-development path\n- invite codes and session tokens are omitted from ordinary JSON/list responses and browser persistence\n- invited users and delegated agents cannot receive workspace-admin or sharing-admin authority in v1\n- CLI-backed agent harnesses carry the server-owned Teams subject only through an opaque loopback grant; the subject is cache-bound and is not projected into child environment variables, headers, or plugin-visible context fields\n- logout, expiry, revocation, membership removal, and grant removal are revalidated against open member sessions\n\n## User Impact\n\n- Workspace owners can invite a member and grant exact-tab `read`, `request changes`, or `write` access.\n- Members use a restricted `/teams` portal rather than the privileged operator Control UI.\n- Requests for changes return to the owning user for approval; members and agents cannot become workspace or sharing administrators in v1.\n- The feature remains local and opt-in. It adds no SaaS identity, email delivery, SSO/SCIM, billing, cross-gateway federation, or member access to the normal operator dashboard.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- 611 focused feature assertions pass across six Vitest shards: gateway, CLI, plugin API, Workspaces, and UI\n- 160 MCP grant/tool-resolution assertions pass, plus targeted CLI-subject and loopback domain-cache isolation regressions\n- production core/extensions and core/extensions test TypeScript projects pass\n- scoped oxfmt, oxlint, and `git diff --check` pass\n- bootstrap -> invite -> exact read -> cross-tab denial -> session revocation integration flow passes\n- independent reviews of security, Workspaces semantics, UI privacy, rebase conflict adaptations, and CLI-harness identity propagation found no remaining concrete issues\n\nThe repository-wide UI-test TypeScript project currently reports four strictness errors in unchanged `ui/src/pages/chat/chat-view.test.ts`; the 67 focused Teams/UI assertions pass.\n",
        "branch": "feat/teams-rbac-plugin-api",
